\ Benchmark: the unflagged half of the spliced-quotation bracket pair.
\
\ The loop body is an `if` branch. The compiler splices such a branch inline at the call site.
\ `@has?` cannot install a word, so the may-define analysis clears the branch. It splices bare.
\
\ The flagged twin is quotation_bracket_splice_flagged.1z. It differs in one word. Both natives pop
\ the key and the receiver, extract the key string, and read the same map. One pushes the value and
\ the other a boolean. So the lookup either side pays is the same.
\
\ The loop is a tail-recursive word for AOT compatibility.

test-hash: ( -- h ) const [ H{ k: 1 } ] ;

bench-lookup: ( n -- ) [
  dup 0 = [ drop ] [
    1 - test-hash k: @has? drop bench-lookup
  ] if
] ;

400000 bench-lookup

"done" print-line
